/* visual-sources-data.js: every unit's Visual Sources tab, drawn by visual-sources.js.
   VISUAL_SOURCES[n] = { frame: "HIPP" | "HAPP", items: [ {num, title, source, desc,
     img  (or imgs: [..], imgTitles: [..], imgSources?: [..] for a paired comparison),
     q, o: [4], c, e,                      the AP-style question (c = index of the answer)
     model: {h, a, p, pov} } ] }           exemplar sourcing analysis, one per part
   frame only changes the labels (HIPP: Historical Context, Intended Audience,
   Purpose, Point of View; HAPP: Historical Situation, Intended Audience, Purpose, Point of View). */
window.VISUAL_SOURCES = {
1: { frame: "HAPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"Mansa Musa on the Catalan Atlas, c. 1375","source":"Detail from the Catalan Atlas, a world map made on the Mediterranean island of Majorca around 1375 and attributed to the Jewish mapmaker Abraham Cresques.","desc":"A crowned African king sits on a throne holding a gold scepter in one hand and a large golden nugget or orb in the other. He is drawn over the map of West Africa, surrounded by the lines mapmakers used to plot sailing directions, with a caption in Catalan beside him.","img":"images/tl/u1-tl-5.jpg","q":"The image of Mansa Musa on this European map most directly reflects","o":["Mali’s conquest of Muslim Spain and control of Mediterranean ports","the spread of Christianity in West Africa through Portuguese missionaries","European awareness of West Africa’s gold wealth through trans-Saharan trade","Portuguese trading colonies already established along the West African coast"],"c":2,"e":"News of Mali’s gold, spread by Mansa Musa’s 1324 pilgrimage through Cairo and by trans-Saharan merchants, reached Mediterranean Europe. The map shows him holding gold to mark West Africa as a source of wealth. Portuguese voyages to West Africa began only in the 1400s (D).","model":{"h":"By the 1300s, gold from Mali crossed the Sahara to North Africa and then the Mediterranean, where it supplied European mints. Mansa Musa’s lavish pilgrimage in 1324 made his wealth famous across the Islamic world and beyond.","a":"Wealthy European patrons and rulers. The atlas was a luxury object; it was given to King Charles V of France.","p":"To map the known world, including trade routes and sources of valuable goods, and to show off the mapmaker’s knowledge gathered from merchants and travelers.","pov":"Cresques worked in Majorca, a trading hub where Jewish, Christian, and Muslim merchants exchanged information. He never visited Mali, so the image reflects what Mediterranean traders said about it, and it shows Mansa Musa in European royal style, with a crown and scepter."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"Map of Tenochtitlan, 1524","source":"Woodcut map of Tenochtitlan printed in Nuremberg, Germany, in 1524 with a Latin edition of Hernán Cortés’s letters to Emperor Charles V.","desc":"A round island city in the middle of a lake, crowded with buildings and connected to the shore by long causeways. At the center is a large walled square labeled in Latin as the temple where sacrifices take place. A flag with the Habsburg double-headed eagle flies at the top left, and a map of the Gulf of Mexico coast appears on the left.","img":"images/tl/u1-tl-6.jpg","q":"The map is best used as evidence of","o":["the layout of Tenochtitlan as drawn by Aztec mapmakers before contact","European views of Tenochtitlan’s size and order soon after the conquest","the Inca road system that linked Cuzco to the provinces of the Andes","the decline of Aztec cities from drought before the Spanish arrived"],"c":1,"e":"The map was printed in Germany to accompany Cortés’s letters, so it shows how Europeans pictured the city from Spanish reports. It reflects the city’s real size, causeways, and central temple precinct, but through European eyes and artistic conventions.","model":{"h":"Cortés and his Native allies conquered Tenochtitlan in 1521. Printed copies of his letters spread news of the conquest across Europe, while the Spanish began building Mexico City on the ruins.","a":"Educated European readers curious about the newly encountered lands, along with the Habsburg court that ruled Spain and the Holy Roman Empire.","p":"To illustrate Cortés’s account and show readers the size and wealth of the city Spain had won, which also glorified the conquest.","pov":"The mapmaker worked from Spanish descriptions, not personal observation. European-style buildings and the Habsburg flag show the city through a conqueror’s eyes, and labeling the center as a place of sacrifice emphasizes what Europeans found shocking."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"The Qutb Minar, Delhi, begun c. 1199","source":"Photograph of the Qutb Minar in Delhi, India, a stone tower begun around 1199 by Qutb al-Din Aibak, who later founded the Delhi Sultanate, and completed by his successor Iltutmish; its top stories were rebuilt in the late 1300s. Beside it stand ruins of the Quwwat ul-Islam mosque.","desc":"A tall, tapering tower of red and buff sandstone with fluted sides and several balconies rises above the ruins of a stone arch. The tower is decorated with bands of carved inscriptions.","img":"images/tl/u1-tl-3.jpg","q":"The building of the monument shown is best understood as","o":["evidence that Hindu kingdoms still ruled northern India in the 1200s","a monument built by the Mughal Empire to celebrate its victory at Panipat","a Buddhist pilgrimage site built by rulers who opposed the Delhi Sultanate","Muslim rulers displaying the power and faith of a new Islamic state"],"c":3,"e":"The Turkic rulers who founded the Delhi Sultanate in 1206 used monumental architecture to proclaim their victory and their faith. The Mughals (B) came three centuries later, in 1526.","model":{"h":"Turkic Muslim armies conquered much of northern India in the late 1100s. Qutb al-Din Aibak became the first sultan of Delhi in 1206. The nearby mosque was built partly with stone taken from demolished Hindu and Jain temples.","a":"The people of Delhi, most of them Hindu, and the sultan’s Muslim soldiers and officials. The tower could be seen from far away.","p":"To celebrate the victory of the new rulers, proclaim Islam, and possibly to serve as a minaret for the call to prayer.","pov":"The tower reflects the perspective of the conquering rulers, presenting their power as permanent. It does not show how the Hindu majority viewed the new rulers, so historians need other sources for that."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"Along the River During the Qingming Festival (Qing court version)","source":"Detail from the Qing court version of Along the River During the Qingming Festival, painted by court artists in 1737, based on a twelfth-century Song dynasty scroll by Zhang Zeduan.","desc":"A busy riverside landscape: crowds gather around an outdoor stage where a performance is taking place, boats carry passengers and cargo on the river, travelers walk the roads with goods on shoulder poles, and farm fields, shops, and tile-roofed buildings spread across the countryside.","img":"images/tl/u1-tl-1.jpg","q":"A historian using this image as evidence about Song dynasty society should be most cautious because","o":["it was painted in the 1700s, so some details may reflect Qing rather than Song life","it was painted by European missionaries who never actually visited China themselves","it shows only military parades and leaves out the daily life of ordinary people","Song cities were too small to have the markets and shops that the scroll shows"],"c":0,"e":"This version was painted for the Qing court in 1737, about six centuries after Zhang Zeduan’s Song original. It is good evidence of how the Qing remembered the Song, but its details (clothing, buildings, entertainments) may be Qing.","model":{"h":"Zhang Zeduan’s original scroll showed the bustling commercial life of the Song capital around the early 1100s. Later dynasties admired and copied it; the Qing emperor Qianlong’s court produced this version in 1737.","a":"The Qing emperor and his court, who collected and admired art celebrating Chinese prosperity.","p":"To honor a famous Song masterpiece while presenting an idealized picture of a peaceful, prosperous, well-governed China, a message that also flattered the Qing rulers.","pov":"The court painters worked for the emperor, so the scene is orderly and prosperous, with no poverty or unrest. Because it is a later version, it shows how the Qing imagined the Song past, not a direct record of Song life."}}
]},
2: { frame: "HIPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"The Catalan Atlas: Asia and the Silk Roads (1375)","source":"Panel from the Catalan Atlas, attributed to Cresques Abraham, a Jewish mapmaker of Majorca, 1375.","desc":"A section of a lavish European world map showing the cities, enthroned rulers, and caravan routes of Asia, knowledge Europeans gained largely through Silk Road trade and travelers like Marco Polo.","img":"images/u2-vis-1.jpg","q":"The map is best used as evidence for which of the following in the period c. 1200–1450?","o":["European states had established direct colonial rule over much of Asia","Long-distance trade routes spread knowledge of distant regions across Afro-Eurasia","Most Europeans had accurate firsthand knowledge of East Asian geography","Sea routes had completely replaced overland trade across Eurasia"],"c":1,"e":"The atlas reflects how Silk Road commerce and travelers' accounts transmitted knowledge of Asia's cities, rulers, and goods to Europe, even though the mapmaker in Majorca had never been there. It shows the cultural and intellectual consequences of Afro-Eurasian trade connectivity.","model":{"h":"The atlas was made in 1375 in Majorca, a Mediterranean crossroads, during the Pax Mongolica when overland routes were relatively safe and European merchants and travelers were reaching Asia in growing numbers.","a":"It was created for the royal court of Aragon, a wealthy, elite audience interested in trade, geography, and the riches of the East, not for ordinary people.","p":"Its purpose was both practical and prestigious: to compile the best available geographic and commercial knowledge and to display the sophistication and reach of its patrons.","pov":"As a Majorcan Jewish cartographer drawing on Arab, Jewish, and European sources, Cresques blended many traditions, but his knowledge of inland Asia was secondhand, so the map mixes real trade information with legend."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"A Persian Court Under a Royal Parasol","source":"Persian manuscript illustration showing Takash, Sultan of Khwarazm, receiving a courtier; Persianate world, c. 14th century.","desc":"An enthroned Central Asian Muslim ruler sits beneath an ornate parasol as a bowing courtier and attendants approach, an image of the wealthy, sophisticated Islamic courts of Central Asia that were tied into Silk Road trade and later conquered by the Mongols.","img":"images/u2-vis-2.jpg","q":"This image best reflects which feature of Afro-Eurasia before the Mongol conquests?","o":["The absence of centralized states across Central Asia","The wealth and courtly sophistication of Islamic states linked to Silk Road trade","The dominance of Christianity across Central Asia","The rejection of settled life by all Central Asian peoples"],"c":1,"e":"The Khwarazmian empire was a prosperous Islamic state enriched by its position astride the Silk Roads. Its refined court culture: reflected in fine manuscript painting, shows the sophistication of the Central Asian world that Mongol expansion would soon absorb into a single trade network.","model":{"h":"The image comes from the Persianate Islamic world; Khwarazm was a powerful Central Asian empire on the Silk Roads that Genghis Khan destroyed in the 1220s.","a":"Illustrated manuscripts like this were made for rulers and wealthy patrons who could afford them, not for a mass audience.","p":"Its purpose was to glorify the ruler and illustrate courtly power and refinement, presenting the sultan as majestic and legitimate.","pov":"As court-sponsored art, it offers an idealized, flattering view of royal authority and reveals nothing about ordinary subjects or the empire's weaknesses."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"Burying the Dead During the Black Death (1349)","source":"Illustration from the chronicle of Gilles li Muisis showing citizens of Tournai burying plague victims, 1349.","desc":"Townspeople place plague victims into coffins for burial during the Black Death, which spread along the same trade routes that connected Afro-Eurasia.","img":"images/u2-vis-3.jpg","q":"The spread of the disease shown here was most directly a consequence of which of the following?","o":["The expansion of interregional trade and travel networks","The collapse of all long-distance trade in the 13th century","European colonization of the Americas","The disappearance of cities across Afro-Eurasia"],"c":0,"e":"The Black Death spread from Asia to the Middle East and Europe along the Silk Road and maritime routes revived under the Mongols. It is a classic example of the biological consequences of Afro-Eurasian connectivity, the same networks that carried goods and ideas also carried disease.","model":{"h":"This was drawn in 1349, at the peak of the Black Death, which killed perhaps a third of Europe's population and devastated much of Afro-Eurasia.","a":"As part of a civic and religious chronicle, it was intended for a literate, often religious audience recording the catastrophe for posterity.","p":"Its purpose was to document and memorialize the scale of death, perhaps framing the plague in moral or religious terms.","pov":"Drawn from a European Christian perspective, it focuses on the communal experience of mass death rather than on the disease's Asian origins or how trade transmitted it."}}
]},
3: { frame: "HIPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"Ottoman Janissaries with Muskets at the Siege of Rhodes (1522)","source":"Ottoman miniature depicting Janissaries firing muskets during the Siege of Rhodes, 1522.","desc":"Elite Ottoman Janissary infantry fire gunpowder muskets at fortified walls defended by the Knights of St. John: a scene of the firearms that made the Ottomans a leading gunpowder empire.","img":"images/u3-vis-1.jpg","q":"The image best illustrates which development in the period 1450–1750?","o":["The use of gunpowder weapons to build and expand large empires","The decline of infantry in favor of cavalry warfare","The rejection of firearms by Islamic empires","The purely naval basis of Ottoman military power"],"c":0,"e":"The Ottomans, Safavids, and Mughals are called gunpowder empires because they used cannons and firearms to conquer rivals and centralize power. The disciplined, musket-armed Janissaries were central to Ottoman military dominance.","model":{"h":"Made in the Ottoman Empire in the 16th century, when the Ottomans were expanding aggressively and the Janissaries were the most feared infantry in Europe and the Middle East.","a":"As a manuscript illustration, it was created for the sultan and elite court audience to celebrate Ottoman victories.","p":"Its purpose was to glorify Ottoman military power and commemorate a triumph, not to give an objective account of the battle.","pov":"From an official Ottoman perspective, it emphasizes Ottoman strength and discipline and portrays the campaign as a heroic success."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"The Enthronement of Sultan Suleiman I","source":"Ottoman miniature depicting the enthronement of Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent, 16th century.","desc":"Suleiman I sits on a golden throne beneath a canopy, surrounded by turbaned officials and viziers, an image designed to display the sultan's supreme authority and the hierarchy of the Ottoman state.","img":"images/u3-vis-2.jpg","q":"This image best reflects how land-based empires in this period sought to:","o":["Share power equally among competing noble families","Project the ruler's legitimacy and centralized authority","Replace religion with secular government","Limit the ruler's power through written constitutions"],"c":1,"e":"Rulers of land-based empires used ceremony, art, and imagery to legitimize centralized power. The composition: the sultan elevated and enthroned above his officials, visually communicates a strict hierarchy with the ruler at its apex.","model":{"h":"Produced during or after the reign of Suleiman (r. 1520–1566), the height of Ottoman wealth and power.","a":"Made for the court and the sultan himself; the audience was the imperial elite.","p":"Its purpose was to legitimize and glorify the sultan's rule, presenting his authority as majestic and God-given.","pov":"As court-commissioned art, it offers an idealized, flattering image of royal power rather than a neutral record."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"The Devshirme: Recruiting Boys in the Balkans","source":"Miniature from the Süleymanname depicting the devshirme, the collection of Christian boys for imperial service, 16th century.","desc":"An Ottoman official registers Christian boys (in red) taken from Balkan families, who look on. These recruits would be converted to Islam and trained as administrators or elite Janissary soldiers.","img":"images/u3-vis-3.jpg","q":"The system depicted here is best understood as a method by which the Ottoman Empire:","o":["Recruited and trained loyal administrators and soldiers for the state","Expelled religious minorities from the empire","Encouraged voluntary migration to the capital","Granted local Christian lords full autonomy"],"c":0,"e":"The devshirme took Christian boys from the Balkans, converted them, and trained them as bureaucrats or Janissaries loyal directly to the sultan. It was a distinctive Ottoman method of building a capable, dependent administrative and military class.","model":{"h":"Created in the 16th century, when the devshirme was a central institution of Ottoman administration and the empire ruled a large Christian population in the Balkans.","a":"As official court art, it was meant for the sultan and elite, presenting the system as orderly and legitimate.","p":"Its purpose was to document and normalize a key institution of imperial governance.","pov":"From the Ottoman state's perspective the scene appears orderly and dignified; it does not show the perspective of the families losing their sons."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"The Taj Mahal, Agra","source":"The Taj Mahal, a mausoleum commissioned by Mughal emperor Shah Jahan, completed c. 1653 (modern photograph).","desc":"A vast white marble mausoleum with a central dome, minarets, and formal gardens, built by Shah Jahan for his wife, one of the most famous examples of Mughal imperial architecture.","img":"images/u3-vis-4.jpg","q":"Monumental structures like the one shown were most often used by rulers to:","o":["Provide practical housing for the urban poor","Display imperial wealth, power, and legitimacy","Serve mainly as fortifications against invasion","Replace religious institutions with secular ones"],"c":1,"e":"Rulers of land-based empires used monumental art and architecture to project wealth, power, and legitimacy. The Taj Mahal blends Persian, Islamic, and Indian styles and demonstrated the Mughal dynasty's resources and grandeur.","model":{"h":"Built in the mid-17th century at the height of Mughal wealth under Shah Jahan.","a":"Its audience was both contemporaries and posterity, subjects, rivals, and future generations who would associate the monument with Mughal greatness.","p":"Its purpose was to memorialize the emperor's wife and, at the same time, glorify the power and legitimacy of the dynasty.","pov":"As an imperial commission, it reflects the perspective and priorities of the Mughal ruling elite and their command of vast resources and labor."}}
]},
4: { frame: "HIPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"Casta Painting: The Racial Hierarchy of Spanish America","source":"Casta painting, colonial Spanish America (New Spain), 18th century.","desc":"A grid of labeled scenes showing families of different racial mixtures (for example, 'De Español y India, Mestizo'), each combination given a specific name, a visual catalog of the colonial casta hierarchy.","img":"images/u4-vis-1.jpg","q":"The painting is best used as evidence of which development after 1450?","o":["The creation of new, racially based social hierarchies in colonial societies","The complete social equality of colonial populations","The absence of European settlers in the Americas","The abolition of slavery across Spanish America"],"c":0,"e":"The mixing of European, Indigenous, and African peoples in the Americas produced new social hierarchies. The casta system ranked people by ancestry, with peninsulares at the top, and reflects how colonial societies were reorganized around race.","model":{"h":"Painted in 18th-century Spanish America, where a complex mixed-race society had developed over two centuries of colonization.","a":"Casta paintings were often made for elite Spanish and European audiences curious about colonial society, sometimes to be sent back to Spain.","p":"Their purpose was to categorize and display the colonial racial order as neat and rule-bound, imposing order on a far messier reality.","pov":"They reflect an elite Spanish colonial perspective that treated race as fixed and hierarchical and presented that hierarchy as natural and legitimate."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"Diagram of the Slave Ship Brookes","source":"'Description of a Slave Ship,' a diagram of the British slave ship Brookes, produced by abolitionists, 1788.","desc":"An engineering-style diagram showing hundreds of enslaved Africans packed side by side into the decks of a slave ship, created by abolitionists to expose the horrors of the Middle Passage.","img":"images/u4-vis-2.jpg","q":"The image is best used as evidence of which of the following?","o":["The humane treatment of enslaved people during ocean voyages","The brutal conditions of the transatlantic slave trade","The decline of the Atlantic slave trade before 1750","The use of enslaved labor only within Europe"],"c":1,"e":"The diagram documents how enslaved Africans were packed into ships during the Middle Passage. The Atlantic slave trade forcibly transported millions of Africans to labor on American plantations and mines, and this image became a powerful abolitionist tool.","model":{"h":"Produced in 1788 in Britain, during the growing movement to abolish the slave trade.","a":"It was aimed at the British public and Parliament to build support for abolition.","p":"Its purpose was to shock and persuade viewers of the inhumanity of the slave trade in order to change the law.","pov":"Created by abolitionists, it deliberately emphasizes the horror and inhumanity of the trade to advance their political cause."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"A Silver Mine at Potosí","source":"'A Section of a Silver Mine in Potosí,' engraving published in the Universal Magazine, London, 18th century.","desc":"A cross-section of the Cerro Rico mountain at Potosí showing laborers climbing ladders and digging deep underground to extract silver ore, with a refining town in the background.","img":"images/u4-vis-3.jpg","q":"The silver produced by the labor shown here most directly contributed to which of the following?","o":["The isolation of the American economy from global trade","The creation of a global economy linking the Americas, Europe, and Asia","The end of coerced labor in the Spanish Empire","The decline of trade with Ming China"],"c":1,"e":"Potosí's silver, extracted largely through coerced Indigenous labor (the mit'a), flowed across the Atlantic and Pacific, much of it ultimately to Ming China, helping create the first truly global economy connected by silver.","model":{"h":"The engraving was published in 18th-century London; Potosí had been the world's greatest silver source since the 16th century.","a":"As a magazine illustration, it was made for a curious European reading public interested in the wider world.","p":"Its purpose was to inform and fascinate European readers about the workings of a famous colonial mine.","pov":"From a European outsider's perspective, it presents the mine as an object of curiosity and does not dwell on the suffering of the coerced Indigenous laborers who worked it."}}
]},
5: { frame: "HIPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"Liberty Leading the People","source":"'Liberty Leading the People,' oil painting by Eugène Delacroix, France, 1830.","desc":"An allegorical woman personifying Liberty holds the French tricolor flag and leads an armed crowd of revolutionaries of different classes over a barricade and fallen bodies.","img":"images/u5-vis-1.jpg","q":"The painting best reflects which force of the long nineteenth century?","o":["The defense of absolute monarchy against reform","Revolutionary and nationalist ideals inspiring political change","The rejection of Enlightenment thought","The decline of popular political participation"],"c":1,"e":"The painting celebrates revolution and the ideals, liberty, popular sovereignty, and nationalism, that inspired the Atlantic Revolutions and later nineteenth-century uprisings. Liberty leading citizens of different classes captures the era's revolutionary spirit.","model":{"h":"Painted in 1830 to commemorate the July Revolution in France, part of a century of revolutions rooted in Enlightenment ideas.","a":"Aimed at a French public and art establishment, it was meant to stir national and revolutionary feeling.","p":"Its purpose was to celebrate and glorify popular revolution and the ideal of liberty.","pov":"Delacroix, sympathetic to the revolution, presents the uprising as heroic and unifying, romanticizing the violence as noble sacrifice."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"The Standard Oil Octopus","source":"American political cartoon depicting the Standard Oil Company as an octopus, c. 1904.","desc":"A giant octopus labeled 'Standard Oil' wraps its tentacles around industries and a state capitol and reaches toward the U.S. Capitol: a cartoon attacking the power of industrial monopolies.","img":"images/u5-vis-2.jpg","q":"The cartoon is best understood as a reaction to which development of the industrial age?","o":["The concentration of economic and political power in giant corporations","The failure of industry to produce any large companies","Government ownership of all major industries","The decline of the oil industry in the United States"],"c":0,"e":"Industrial capitalism produced huge corporations and monopolies like Standard Oil. This cartoon reflects the reaction against their concentrated economic and political power, the kind of criticism that fueled reform movements and regulation.","model":{"h":"Created in the early 1900s in the United States, during the era of massive industrial trusts and Progressive-era calls for reform.","a":"Aimed at a broad newspaper-reading public to shape opinion against monopolies.","p":"Its purpose was to criticize and warn against the power of corporate monopolies and to build support for regulating them.","pov":"From a reform-minded, anti-monopoly perspective, it deliberately portrays the corporation as a menacing, all-consuming threat."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"Coalbrookdale by Night","source":"'Coalbrookdale by Night,' oil painting by Philip James de Loutherbourg, Britain, 1801.","desc":"An ironworks glows fiery red against the night sky, smoke pouring from furnaces, with workers and a horse-drawn cart in the foreground, a dramatic image of early industrial Britain.","img":"images/u5-vis-3.jpg","q":"The scene depicted is best used as evidence of which of the following?","o":["The environmental and industrial transformation of the Industrial Revolution","The persistence of a purely agricultural economy","The decline of manufacturing in Britain","The absence of coal and iron in British industry"],"c":0,"e":"Coalbrookdale was a birthplace of the Industrial Revolution. The blazing ironworks captures the new scale of coal-and-iron industry, and its dramatic environmental impact, as Britain led the world into industrial production.","model":{"h":"Painted in 1801 in Britain, the first industrial nation, as coal-powered iron production was booming.","a":"Aimed at British art audiences both fascinated and unsettled by the new industrial landscape.","p":"Its purpose was partly to awe viewers with the sublime, dramatic power of industry, capturing both its grandeur and its menace.","pov":"As a Romantic artist, Loutherbourg presents industry in dramatic, almost apocalyptic terms, emphasizing its overwhelming visual power rather than the daily lives of workers."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"The Slave Revolt in Saint-Domingue","source":"European engraving depicting the slave revolt in the French colony of Saint-Domingue, which began in 1791.","desc":"Enslaved people rise up in armed revolt against colonists, with a plantation burning in the distance, the uprising that launched the Haitian Revolution.","img":"images/u5-vis-4.jpg","q":"The event depicted led most directly to which of the following?","o":["The establishment of Haiti as an independent state founded by former slaves","The strengthening of slavery throughout the Caribbean","The peaceful negotiation of colonial independence","The restoration of the French monarchy in the Americas"],"c":0,"e":"The 1791 uprising in Saint-Domingue grew into the Haitian Revolution, the only successful large-scale slave revolt in history, which created the independent state of Haiti and applied the revolutionary ideals of liberty and equality to enslaved people themselves.","model":{"h":"The revolt began in 1791 in France's wealthiest colony, inspired in part by the ideals of the French Revolution then underway.","a":"European engravings of the revolt were aimed at audiences back in Europe, many of whom feared slave uprisings.","p":"Its purpose was often to depict the violence of the revolt, sometimes to alarm European viewers about the dangers of rebellion.","pov":"Drawn from a European colonial perspective, such images tend to emphasize the violence and threat of the uprising rather than the enslaved people's demand for freedom."}}
]},
6: { frame: "HIPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"“Going to Work”: Eyre Crowe, 1874","source":"Oil painting by British artist Eyre Crowe, depicting female workers at a Lancashire cotton mill, 1874.","desc":"Female textile workers streaming into a Lancashire cotton mill. Crowe documented factory life in Britain’s industrial heartland during the height of the Industrial Revolution.","img":"images/u6-vis-1.jpg","q":"The painting is best used to illustrate which of the following about the Industrial Revolution?","o":["The Industrial Revolution increased child labor in British cities","The Industrial Revolution drew large numbers of women into factory wage labor","The Industrial Revolution raised average wages for all British workers","The Industrial Revolution was limited to male workers in heavy industry"],"c":1,"e":"The painting depicts women as the primary workforce shown entering the mill. This directly illustrates how industrialization dramatically expanded women’s participation in wage labor, transforming both the economy and gender roles.","model":{"h":"By the 1870s, Lancashire cotton mills employed hundreds of thousands of workers, disproportionately women and children, under difficult conditions that social reformers were beginning to expose and regulate.","a":"Crowe painted for upper- and middle-class British audiences who consumed mill-made textiles but rarely saw the workers who produced them. His documentary style served both artistic and social-reform purposes.","p":"To document the social reality of industrial labor, the human cost of Britain’s textile industry. The painting functions as both art and implicit social commentary on the lives of working-class women.","pov":"As a British artist embedded in the social reform tradition, Crowe presents workers sympathetically, as dignified laborers, not a threat. This shapes how his audience was meant to feel about industrial conditions."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"Berlin Conference on African Affairs, 1884–85","source":"Engraving of the Berlin West Africa Conference, published in the Illustrated London News, 1884.","desc":"European diplomats and officials seated around a table at the Berlin Conference, where 14 European powers negotiated rules for claiming African territory.","img":"images/u6-vis-2.jpg","q":"The Berlin Conference depicted in this image best illustrates which aspect of 19th-century imperialism?","o":["African rulers negotiating with European powers to maintain territorial sovereignty","European industrial competition driving the rapid partition of Africa without African participation","The humanitarian goals of European powers seeking to end the African slave trade","Religious missionary organizations coordinating European colonial expansion"],"c":1,"e":"The Berlin Conference represents the quintessential example of European powers treating Africa as available for claiming. No African representatives were present, the conference established rules entirely for European benefit.","model":{"h":"By 1884, European powers competed intensely for African territory. The Berlin Conference was convened by Bismarck to regulate competition and prevent war among European powers, not to protect African interests.","a":"Published in the Illustrated London News for British middle-class audiences, this image projected the conference as a legitimate, orderly international process, normalizing the partition of an entire continent as routine diplomacy.","p":"To document and legitimize the Berlin Conference as a reasonable diplomatic gathering, presenting the Scramble for Africa as organized, lawful, and beneficial when it was none of these things.","pov":"As a British publication, the Illustrated London News presents European diplomats as authoritative decision-makers, the visual framing excludes the violence and coercion of colonial conquest that followed."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"Emperor Menelik II of Ethiopia, c. 1889","source":"Official portrait photograph of Emperor Menelik II of Ethiopia, c. 1889.","desc":"Formal portrait of Emperor Menelik II, who led Ethiopia to victory at the Battle of Adwa (1896), defeating an Italian colonial army and preserving Ethiopian independence.","img":"images/u6-vis-3.jpg","q":"Menelik II’s leadership most directly challenges which assumption about 19th-century imperialism?","o":["Western-style weapons were necessary for any military victory against European armies","African rulers could not successfully combine diplomatic and military strategies against European powers","Non-Western leaders could mobilize large modern armies and decisively defeat European colonial forces","European imperialism in Africa was always accepted without significant armed resistance"],"c":2,"e":"Menelik defeated a European army through diplomatic skill, military modernization, and effective large-scale mobilization, the opposite of the passive “inferior” African ruler that imperial ideology imagined.","model":{"h":"The Scramble for Africa (1880–1914) produced the assumption that African resistance was futile. Adwa (1896) directly contradicted this: an African emperor defeated a European army in open battle.","a":"Official portraits of Menelik II circulated domestically to project imperial authority and internationally, to European governments who needed to take him seriously as a sovereign equal after Adwa.","p":"To project Menelik’s authority and legitimacy as a modern sovereign ruler, not the primitive “chief” that European racial ideology imagined African leaders to be.","pov":"As Ethiopian Emperor, Menelik’s official image was carefully constructed to communicate sovereignty, modernity, and equality with European rulers, a direct visual argument against imperial racial hierarchy."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"“The White Man’s Burden”: Puck Magazine, April 1, 1899","source":"Political cartoon, Puck Magazine, April 1, 1899, depicting Uncle Sam and John Bull carrying colonial subjects up a mountain.","desc":"Uncle Sam (U.S.) and John Bull (Britain) carry baskets labeled with colonial territories up a rugged mountain toward “civilization” while colonial subjects are depicted as children.","img":"images/u6-vis-4.jpg","q":"The cartoon is best used as evidence of which of the following about late-19th-century imperialism?","o":["Western powers unanimously opposed Kipling’s argument about the “civilizing mission”","American imperial ideology used the “civilizing mission” to frame colonial control as uplift for “inferior” peoples","The United States treated its colonial subjects with equal rights and legal protections","American imperialism was motivated purely by strategic military concerns, not racial ideology"],"c":1,"e":"The cartoon reflects the “civilizing mission” ideology, Western powers as guides, colonial subjects as burdens to be uplifted. The racial hierarchy embedded in the imagery reveals how deeply racial assumptions structured imperial ideology.","model":{"h":"Published in April 1899 amid American debate over annexing the Philippines, the cartoon appeared as the Senate ratified the Treaty of Paris and the U.S. officially became a colonial power.","a":"Puck’s middle-class American audience was the target, the cartoon persuades readers that colonial annexation was moral obligation, not conquest, by framing it as shared Anglo-American duty.","p":"To persuade American and British audiences to support colonial annexation by framing it as civilizational mission, the “burden” framing makes conquest appear as sacrifice rather than exploitation.","pov":"As an American satirical magazine commenting on imperial politics, Puck reflects the pro-imperialist mainstream of 1899. The racial hierarchy in the image reveals how deeply racial ideology structured American views of colonial subjects."}}
]},
7: { frame: "HIPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"“The Hero”: George Grosz, 1936","source":"Lithograph by German artist George Grosz, 1936","desc":"A satirical lithograph depicting a helmeted, grotesque soldier figure in the expressionist style. Grosz was a German satirist who fled Nazi Germany in 1933.","img":"images/stimuli/page-1.jpg?v=2","q":"Which of the following best describes the 1936 lithograph (entitled The Hero) by German artist George Grosz?","o":["A propaganda poster prepared by the Nazi Party","A protest poster against the atrocities of the atomic bomb","A representation of a Holocaust victim","A post-First World War print expressing antiwar sentiment"],"c":3,"e":"Grosz consistently expressed outrage at militarism. Having witnessed WWI’s horrors and fled Nazi Germany, he used satirical art to condemn the glorification of war, making this an antiwar image, not pro-war propaganda.","model":{"h":"WWI (1914–18) killed 20 million people and shattered European confidence in militarism. By 1936, Hitler was remilitarizing Germany, the same militarist ideology Grosz spent his career attacking. Antiwar art was increasingly dangerous to produce in Europe.","a":"Anti-militarist audiences in Europe and the United States, those who opposed the glorification of war and feared the rising tide of fascism in the 1930s.","p":"To satirize and condemn the glorification of military heroes, to expose the brutal, dehumanizing reality of warfare behind the propaganda-driven celebration of soldiers.","pov":"As a German who lived through WWI and fled Nazi persecution, Grosz was personally motivated to use art as political protest. His point of view is that of a traumatized witness turned critic of the state that produced him."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"Aeroflot Soviet Airline Advertisement, 1950s","source":"Advertisement by Aeroflot, a Soviet state-owned commercial airline, used in the Soviet Union during the 1950s.","desc":"A commercial advertisement showing a Soviet jetliner flying over major international landmarks from America, Europe, Africa, and Asia, suggesting Soviet global reach.","img":"images/stimuli/page-2.jpg?v=2","q":"The image can best be used to illustrate which of the following about communist states?","o":["The collectivization of agriculture","Limited technological progress in comparison with Western states","State control of borders with noncommunist states","State control over the economy"],"c":3,"e":"Aeroflot was a state-owned enterprise. The advertisement demonstrates that the Soviet state directly controlled commercial aviation, a sector privately owned in capitalist economies, as part of broader central economic planning.","model":{"h":"The 1950s were the height of the Cold War. The USSR competed with the West in technology and global prestige (Sputnik launched 1957). Soviet state planning claimed to outperform Western capitalism in economic development and technological achievement.","a":"Soviet citizens and potentially foreign audiences, those the Soviet state wanted to persuade that socialist planning could achieve technological and commercial success equal to or exceeding the West.","p":"To promote state-owned airline service while projecting Soviet technological achievement, modernity, and global connectivity, serving double duty as both commercial advertising and political propaganda.","pov":"As a state-controlled enterprise, Aeroflot’s advertising directly reflected Soviet government priorities. The image cannot be separated from Soviet Cold War ideology: global reach = ideological reach."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"Workers and Soldiers in Saint Petersburg, February 1917","source":"Photograph: workers and soldiers marching in Saint Petersburg after taking control of the city, February 1917 (Keystone-France/Contributor).","desc":"A large crowd of both civilian workers and uniformed soldiers marching together through the streets of Saint Petersburg, the beginning of the February Revolution.","img":"images/stimuli/page-3.jpg?v=2","q":"The makeup of the force shown in the image is best explained by the fact that by 1917","o":["nationalism in Russia had intensified sharply as a result of the shared experiences of the First World War","new military technologies had made it possible to transport soldiers more rapidly","wartime propaganda had fueled fanatical loyalty to the Tsarist regime among ordinary Russians","Russia’s army morale and home-front effort were collapsing because of the strains of total war"],"c":3,"e":"By early 1917 Russia had suffered millions of casualties, food shortages, and military defeats. Total war’s demands had broken both military discipline and civilian loyalty to the Tsar, soldiers and workers united against the regime.","model":{"h":"Russia entered WWI in 1914 and suffered catastrophic losses: millions dead, armies in retreat, cities starving. The strain of total war collapsed the Tsarist government’s legitimacy. By February 1917, neither the army nor the working class would continue to fight for the Tsar.","a":"The photograph was taken by a photojournalist for historical/news documentation, not staged for a specific audience, but intended to record the revolutionary moment as it unfolded.","p":"To document the February Revolution of 1917, the moment when Russian soldiers and workers united against the Tsarist regime. This photograph captured the beginning of the end of the Romanov dynasty.","pov":"The crucial insight is the presence of BOTH workers AND soldiers together. The army was supposed to suppress revolution, not join it. Their unity proves that total war had destroyed loyalty to the regime from within."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"Indian Muslim Troops Praying in Surrey, England, 1916","source":"Indian Muslim troops in the British Armed Forces praying. Photograph taken in Surrey, England, 1916 (FPG/Staff). British civilian women are visible watching in the background.","desc":"Indian Muslim soldiers in British military uniforms performing prayer in England, observed by British civilian women in the background.","img":"images/stimuli/page-4.jpg?v=2","q":"The situation shown in the image is best understood in the context of which of the following aspects of twentieth-century warfare?","o":["States used propaganda to intensify patriotism in times of war.","States used new industrial technologies to fight wars that were deadlier and more expensive.","States made full use of their populations and material resources to fight total wars.","States increasingly mobilized their citizens for warfare regardless of gender or class."],"c":2,"e":"The British Indian Army contributed over 1 million soldiers to WWI. Using colonial populations to fight on European soil is direct evidence of total war mobilization, states drawing on every available human and material resource.","model":{"h":"Total war in WWI forced Britain to mobilize all available resources, including the manpower of its empire. By 1916, catastrophic casualties on the Western Front created desperate demand for colonial troops. Over 1 million Indian soldiers served in WWI.","a":"British home-front civilian audiences (note women watching) and potentially Indian colonial audiences, to demonstrate imperial unity and the shared sacrifice of the war effort across the empire.","p":"Documentary/propaganda: to record and publicize the diverse composition of British imperial forces; to build home-front support for the war by showing the empire fighting together.","pov":"The British civilian women watching frame the soldiers as both foreign and fascinating, the home front literally encountering the empire. The image reveals how total war made the colonial relationship visible to ordinary British people in ways peacetime never had."}},
  {"num":5,"title":"David Olère: “The Food of the Dead for the Living”, c. 1950","source":"David Olère, French Jewish painter and Auschwitz inmate (March 1943–May 1945). “The Food of the Dead for the Living,” painted circa 1950.","desc":"A painting by Holocaust survivor David Olère depicting the Auschwitz crematoria system, the industrial apparatus of mass murder that he was forced to work within as a Sonderkommando prisoner.","img":"images/stimuli/page-5.jpg?v=2","q":"The implementation of the policies of extermination shown in the image is most directly explained by which of the following aspects of Nazi ideology?","o":["The idea that Germans descended from a master “Aryan” race","The idea that some minority populations could eventually be Germanized","The idea that minority populations within Germany were somehow responsible for its defeat during the First World War","The idea that Germany needed to expand its postwar borders in order to provide “living space” for its people"],"c":2,"e":"Nazi ideology used the “stab in the back” legend to blame Jews for Germany’s WWI defeat. This scapegoating made Jews existential threats to the state, the ideological justification for organized extermination.","model":{"h":"The Holocaust (1941–45) was the state-organized murder of ~6 million Jews and 6 million others. Olère survived Auschwitz as a Sonderkommando, forced to operate the crematoria. He painted from memory after liberation, driven by the need to document what he witnessed.","a":"Post-war audiences: survivors, historians, and anyone at risk of denying or minimizing the Holocaust. Painted circa 1950, when the world was still processing the scale of Nazi atrocities.","p":"To bear witness: to ensure the atrocities of the Holocaust were documented, visualized, and impossible to deny. Olère felt a moral obligation as a survivor to testify through art.","pov":"Olère did not merely observe, he was forced to participate in the camp’s operations. His paintings have the authority of direct testimony. As HAPP analysis: his point of view as a victim and forced participant means the source cannot be dismissed as exaggerated or second-hand."}},
  {"num":6,"title":"German East African Troops (Askari), WWI","source":"Photograph of German East African troops (Askari soldiers) serving under German command during the First World War.","desc":"African soldiers (Askari troops) in military formation, serving under German officers in German East Africa (modern Tanzania) during WWI.","img":"images/stimuli/page-6.jpg?v=2","q":"The photograph above of German East African troops best illustrates which of the following historical processes during the First World War?","o":["The increasing inclusion of non-Europeans in European conflicts","The undermining of European claims to moral superiority as a result of the destructiveness of the First World War","The development of African nationalism as a consequence of Africans’ involvement in European wars","The gradual extension of self-rule to Europe’s African colonies in the face of anticolonial protests by Africans"],"c":0,"e":"Both the Entente and Central Powers mobilized African and Asian colonial troops to fight WWI. German East Africa fielded Askari soldiers; Britain used over 1 million Indian soldiers. This demonstrates WWI as a truly global, inclusive conflict.","model":{"h":"European colonial powers mobilized troops from Africa, Asia, and other colonized regions. German East Africa (Tanganyika) was defended largely by African Askari troops under German officers, and held out until after the Armistice in 1918, making it the last theater of WWI to surrender.","a":"Likely German military or colonial documentation, intended to showcase German colonial military organization and the capability of African troops under German command.","p":"Military documentation of colonial forces; possibly propaganda to demonstrate German colonial administration and African soldier loyalty, both for German domestic audiences and colonial authorities.","pov":"Taken from a German colonial perspective, the photograph portrays Askari soldiers as disciplined assets of the German colonial system, not as individuals with their own agency or political consciousness. The framing reflects the colonial gaze of its time."}},
  {"num":7,"title":"Two Maps: Southeast Asian Decolonization","source":"Two comparative maps of Southeast Asia, showing political boundaries under European colonial rule and after WWII-era decolonization.","desc":"A before-and-after map comparison showing Southeast Asia under European colonial control and then as newly independent nations, particularly highlighting Indonesian independence.","img":"images/stimuli/page-7.jpg?v=2","q":"Which of the following twentieth-century developments most directly weakened European colonial states and contributed to the changes between the two maps?","o":["The decline of European economies during the Great Depression","Japanese occupation of Southeast Asia during the Second World War","Competition between Great Britain and France","The conquest of Spanish imperial territories by the United States"],"c":1,"e":"Japanese occupation (1941–45) dismantled European colonial administrations across Southeast Asia, ended the myth of European invincibility, and in many cases armed independence movements. Countries like Indonesia used the post-war vacuum to declare independence.","model":{"h":"Japan rapidly conquered most of Southeast Asia in 1941–42, overthrowing Dutch, British, French, and American colonial administrations. Japan’s defeat of European powers destroyed the myth that Europeans were naturally superior rulers, accelerating independence movements across the region.","a":"Students and historians comparing political geography over time, the maps are analytical tools for visualizing decolonization as a historical process.","p":"To make the scale of decolonization immediately visible through comparison. The before/after format reveals what decades of historical change look like on a map, turning an abstract process into a concrete visual shift.","pov":"Maps appear neutral but embed political reality as normal. The first map treats colonial boundaries as natural; the second treats national sovereignty as the new norm. The shift between them encodes the entire story of 20th-century decolonization without a single word of text."}}
]},
8: { frame: "HAPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"Aeroflot Soviet Airline Advertisement, 1950s","source":"Advertisement by Aeroflot, a Soviet State-Owned Commercial Airline. The Advertisement Was Used in the Soviet Union During the 1950s. Shawshots / Alamy Stock Photo.","desc":"A Soviet commercial advertisement showing a jet airliner marked “CCCP” (USSR) flying over major international landmarks, the Eiffel Tower, Big Ben, Egyptian pyramids, the Kremlin, American skyscrapers, and an Asian pagoda, with “АЭРОФЛОТ” (Aeroflot) printed across the bottom.","img":"images/stimuli/page-2.jpg","q":"In addition to its main use as advertising material, the image was most likely also intended to","o":["show the Soviet Union’s rise as a major technological power","demonstrate Soviet support for communist parties in Western European countries","encourage improved relations between the United States and the Soviet Union","facilitate the spread of communist ideas in East and Southeast Asia"],"c":0,"e":"The advertisement’s depiction of a speeding Soviet jet aircraft flying over global landmarks was designed to show the Soviet Union’s ability to compete with, and surpass: American and European technological capabilities. During the Cold War, technological achievement (Sputnik, jet aviation, nuclear weapons) was a key arena of superpower competition.","model":{"h":"The 1950s were the height of early Cold War competition. The USSR was racing to match Western technological prestige: the Sputnik satellite launched in 1957; Soviet jet development competed directly with Western aviation. State-owned enterprises like Aeroflot served double duty as commercial services and propaganda tools projecting Soviet modernity.","a":"Soviet citizens: and potentially international audiences: whom the Soviet state wanted to convince that socialist central planning could achieve technological and commercial success equal to or surpassing the capitalist West. The global landmarks in the image signal the international message.","p":"To promote Aeroflot’s airline service while simultaneously projecting Soviet technological achievement, global reach, and modernity. The ad serves two purposes at once: sell tickets and demonstrate that the USSR is a world-class power.","pov":"As a state-controlled enterprise, Aeroflot’s advertising directly reflected Soviet government Cold War priorities. The ad cannot be separated from Soviet ideology: the jet marked “CCCP” dominating the skies above world landmarks encodes the message that Soviet technology is superior, and global."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"Proxy War in Angola, 1975","source":"Left: Cuban soldiers deployed to Angola in support of the MPLA, 1975. Right: South African troops deployed to Angola in support of UNITA/FNLA, 1975. Both AP/Wide World Photos.","desc":"Two photographs taken in Angola during its civil war following independence from Portugal in 1975. The left image shows Cuban soldiers, deployed by Fidel Castro in support of the MPLA (People's Movement for the Liberation of Angola). The right image shows South African soldiers, deployed with US backing in support of UNITA and FNLA forces opposing the MPLA.","imgs":["images/stimuli/u8-2a.jpg","images/stimuli/u8-2b.jpg"],"imgTitles":["Cuban Troops, Angola, 1975","South African Troops, Angola, 1975"],"imgSources":["AP/Wide World Photos","AP/Wide World Photos"],"q":"The intervention of Cuba and South Africa in Angola in 1975 is best understood as an example of","o":["direct military conflict between the United States and Soviet Union during the Cold War","the use of proxy forces by superpowers to advance Cold War geopolitical goals in newly independent states","the Non-Aligned Movement's strategy of recruiting military allies from both Cold War blocs","African nations' successful resistance to all forms of outside intervention after decolonization"],"c":1,"e":"Neither the US nor USSR sent their own troops to Angola. Instead, they backed opposing local factions, the USSR and Cuba supported the MPLA; the US (via the CIA) and South Africa backed UNITA/FNLA. This proxy war pattern: fighting Cold War battles through local allies, repeated across Asia, Africa, and Latin America.","model":{"h":"Angola gained independence from Portugal in November 1975. Three rival movements: MPLA (Soviet-backed), FNLA, and UNITA (US/South Africa-backed): immediately fought for control. The Cold War context transformed a domestic power struggle into an internationalized proxy conflict at the height of superpower competition in Africa.","a":"These press photographs were intended for Western and international news audiences seeking to understand the Angolan Civil War. The images document foreign military presence, implicitly raising questions about post-independence sovereignty.","p":"To document and report on foreign military intervention in a newly independent African state. The photographs serve as historical evidence of how Cold War rivalries extended into decolonized Africa.","pov":"AP photojournalists operated independently of either superpower, but Cold War framing shaped how Western editors captioned and used the images. Cuban presence was often portrayed as Soviet-directed; South African presence was treated as anti-communist resistance, reflecting the ideological lens of Western Cold War media."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"Indian Muslim Soldiers at Prayer, Surrey, England, 1916","source":"Photograph of Indian Muslim soldiers at Friday prayers at a military camp, Surrey, England, 1916. Imperial War Museum, London.","desc":"A photograph of Indian Muslim soldiers performing Friday prayers at a British military camp in Surrey, England during World War I. Indian soldiers served across multiple theaters of WWI, Western Front, Mesopotamia, East Africa, and Gallipoli, with over 1.5 million Indians serving in the British imperial military forces.","img":"images/stimuli/page-4.jpg","q":"Which of the following best explains how the experience of Indian soldiers depicted in this photograph contributed to anti-imperial sentiment after World War I?","o":["Indian soldiers refused to fight for Britain and instead organized armed resistance to colonial rule during the war","The British government formally promised Indian independence in exchange for Indian military service, then broke this promise","Indian soldiers' exposure to European battlefields convinced them that European civilization was morally superior","Indian soldiers who fought for Britain returned home with military skills and political awareness that strengthened nationalist movements and exposed the contradiction of fighting for \"freedom\" while remaining colonized"],"c":3,"e":"Indian veterans returned with military training, cross-cultural exposure, and heightened political consciousness. Having fought and died for the British Empire's stated ideals of liberty and democracy, they found continued colonial subjugation intolerable. The Montagu Declaration (1917) promised Indian self-governance but delivered little. The Amritsar Massacre (1919) radicalized many veterans. The INC and Muslim League both grew significantly in the postwar years.","model":{"h":"Britain mobilized its colonial empire extensively during WWI. India provided 1.5 million soldiers, essential supplies, and financial support. In exchange, Indian political leaders expected meaningful movement toward self-governance. Wartime service intensified both loyalty to British institutions and resentment of continued colonial rule.","a":"This photograph was likely produced for British domestic audiences, to demonstrate imperial loyalty and the diversity of the British war effort. It also served as a morale document for Indian soldiers' families. The framing emphasizes religious practice being accommodated, signaling British respect for Indian culture.","p":"To document Indian military service and loyalty to the British Empire, and to project an image of a multi-ethnic, tolerant empire fighting for civilization. Such images were used in wartime propaganda to justify imperial governance.","pov":"As an Imperial War Museum photograph produced within the British military and propaganda apparatus, this image presents the perspective of the colonial power, not the soldiers themselves. It does not capture the political frustrations, second-class treatment, or growing nationalist ambitions of Indian soldiers serving an empire that denied them self-determination."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"Political Cartoons: Soviet Instability, 1986 & 1989","source":"Left: \"House of Cards,\" published in Krokodil (Soviet satirical magazine), 1986. Right: Western political cartoon depicting Soviet satellite states, 1989.","desc":"Two political cartoons from the late Cold War period. The left image, from the Soviet satirical magazine Krokodil (1986), depicts the Soviet economic or political system as a fragile \"house of cards.\" The right image (1989) shows Soviet satellite states metaphorically marching out of Soviet control, reflecting the wave of revolutions that swept Eastern Europe.","imgs":["images/stimuli/u8-7a.jpg","images/stimuli/u8-7b.jpg"],"imgTitles":["\"House of Cards,\" Soviet political cartoon, 1986","\"Parade,\" Western political cartoon, 1989"],"imgSources":["Krokodil Magazine, USSR","Western press cartoon, 1989"],"q":"Taken together, these two cartoons are best used as evidence of which historical development?","o":["American military pressure forcing the Soviet Union to collapse through economic exhaustion","The Soviet government's successful use of satire to defuse political opposition","Western media's inaccurate prediction of Soviet collapse that was not borne out by events","Rising internal instability and popular discontent within the Soviet bloc that contributed to its eventual dissolution"],"c":3,"e":"By the mid-1980s, structural weaknesses in the Soviet system, economic stagnation, declining legitimacy, restless satellite states, were visible even within Soviet media. Gorbachev's glasnost (openness) policies acknowledged systemic problems. By 1989, those satellite states did break away in rapid succession: Poland, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, East Germany, Romania, and Bulgaria all experienced revolutions. The USSR itself dissolved in 1991.","model":{"h":"Gorbachev came to power in 1985 and launched glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) to reform the Soviet system. Economic stagnation, the Afghanistan war, Chernobyl, and nationalist movements in satellite states all destabilized Soviet authority through the late 1980s.","a":"The Krokodil cartoon addressed Soviet readers who recognized systemic problems but operated within censored media. The Western cartoon addressed international audiences watching Soviet bloc instability. Each cartoon assumes an audience already aware of Soviet fragility.","p":"The Krokodil cartoon, despite being Soviet, used permitted satire to comment on system weaknesses, glasnost expanded the range of permitted criticism. The Western cartoon aimed to document and predict the collapse of Soviet Eastern European empire in real time.","pov":"The perspectives differ sharply: the Soviet cartoon reflects internal acknowledgment of problems within the system (a remarkable concession under glasnost). The Western cartoon reflects external observation with an implicit \"we told you so\" framing about communist instability. Together they show both internal and external recognition of Soviet crisis."}},
  {"num":5,"title":"World Political Map, 1980","source":"World political map showing nation-states, Cold War alignments, and newly independent post-colonial states, 1980. Standard cartographic publication.","desc":"A 1980 world political map showing the distribution of nation-states across the globe. By 1980, most former European colonies in Asia and Africa had achieved independence, producing a dramatically different political geography than the colonial map of 1900. The map reflects decades of decolonization that produced over 80 new sovereign states after 1945.","img":"images/stimuli/u8-8.jpg","q":"A historian studying this map in comparison to a 1900 world map would most likely conclude that newly independent post-colonial states in 1980 were","o":["deeply cautious of both superpowers' intentions, having experienced European imperialism and witnessed Cold War interference in their internal affairs","overwhelmingly aligned with the United States due to American support for decolonization movements during the Cold War","economically prosperous and politically stable due to the institutional frameworks established during the colonial period","fully integrated into Cold War alliances with clear ideological commitments to either capitalism or communism"],"c":0,"e":"Having experienced European colonial domination, newly independent states were wary of any form of external control, from either superpower. The Non-Aligned Movement (founded at Bandung, 1955) reflected this wariness: nations like India, Egypt, Indonesia, and Ghana refused to join either Cold War bloc, recognizing that both superpowers engaged in forms of neo-colonial interference. The 1980 map shows a politically decolonized world that remained economically and strategically contested.","model":{"h":"By 1980, the wave of decolonization was largely complete. Over 80 former colonies had gained independence since 1945. But Cold War competition meant both superpowers sought to influence newly independent states through aid, military support, and proxy conflicts, a new form of external intervention that resembled, in some ways, colonialism.","a":"This map was produced for general educational and reference audiences, students, policymakers, and citizens, to visualize the political geography of the contemporary world. The audience would use it to understand international relations, not to examine colonialism directly.","p":"To document and communicate current political boundaries and national sovereignty. Maps are authoritative documents that reinforce the nation-state as the basic unit of political organization. The 1980 map implicitly legitimizes the post-colonial state system.","pov":"Standard political maps reflect a Western cartographic tradition that treats nation-state borders as natural and legitimate. They do not show colonial boundaries versus indigenous territories, economic inequalities, or the internal divisions created by colonial-era border drawing, all of which shaped the political reality of newly independent states."}},
  {"num":6,"title":"Partition of South Asia, 1947","source":"Map depicting the partition of British India into the independent states of India and Pakistan, August 1947. The map shows the Radcliffe Line boundary and the princely states that acceded to either nation.","desc":"A map showing the partition of British India into the independent nations of India and Pakistan on August 14-15, 1947. The partition was drawn along religious lines, with Muslim-majority areas forming West Pakistan and East Pakistan (later Bangladesh), while Hindu-majority areas formed India. Partition triggered one of history's largest mass migrations, approximately 10-15 million people crossed the new borders, and severe communal violence that killed between 200,000 and 2 million people.","img":"images/stimuli/u8-9.jpg","q":"The partition of British India depicted in this map most directly illustrates which challenge faced by newly independent post-colonial states?","o":["Former colonial powers deliberately drawing borders to create conflict and maintain influence in newly independent territories","The United Nations' decision to intervene in decolonization processes to ensure democratic governance","Newly independent states' difficulty in establishing borders that reflected Cold War alliance commitments","The difficulty of creating stable post-colonial states when colonial rule had governed diverse religious and ethnic communities under a single administrative unit"],"c":3,"e":"The partition of India illustrates how colonial rule created the conditions for post-independence crisis: Britain governed hundreds of millions of Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs as a single administrative unit with no clear path to independent governance that satisfied all communities. Hasty partition along religious lines produced borders that cut across communities, creating mass displacement, violence, and territorial disputes (especially over Kashmir) that persist today. This pattern: colonial administrative units poorly suited to post-colonial governance, repeated across Africa and Asia.","model":{"h":"Britain's decision to withdraw from India was accelerated by postwar exhaustion and the election of a Labour government committed to decolonization. The Mountbatten Plan (June 1947) set partition in motion with a mere 73 days' notice. The speed and violence of partition reflected decades of British divide-and-rule policies that had sharpened Hindu-Muslim political identities.","a":"This map was produced for political leaders, military planners, and the public to communicate the new international boundaries. The Radcliffe Commission drew the actual borders, creating what became known as the \"Radcliffe Line\": named for a man who had never visited India before being asked to partition it.","p":"To establish and communicate sovereign territorial boundaries for the new states of India and Pakistan. Maps of partition served administrative, military, and diplomatic purposes, defining which communities, cities, and resources fell under each nation's jurisdiction.","pov":"Official partition maps reflect the perspective of colonial administrators and the Boundary Commission, not the communities affected. They show lines on paper, not the villages bisected, families separated, or religious sites suddenly placed in the \"wrong\" country. The clinical cartographic perspective obscures the human catastrophe the borders created."}},
  {"num":7,"title":"Africa: Colonial vs. Independent, 1930 and 1981","source":"Left: Map of Africa showing European colonial possessions, 1930. Right: Map of Africa showing independent nation-states, 1981. Historical atlas, standard cartographic publication.","desc":"Two maps showing Africa in 1930 and 1981. In 1930, virtually the entire continent was divided among European colonial powers, Britain, France, Belgium, Portugal, Italy, and Spain. By 1981, all but a few territories had achieved independence, producing a continent of over 50 sovereign nation-states. The transformation occurred primarily between 1956 and 1975, with 1960 alone seeing 17 new African nations.","imgs":["images/stimuli/u8-11a.jpg","images/stimuli/u8-11b.jpg"],"imgTitles":["Colonial Possessions in Africa, 1930","Independent States in Africa, 1981"],"imgSources":["Historical atlas map, 1930","Historical atlas map, 1981"],"q":"The transformation depicted in these two maps was most directly driven by which historical development?","o":["The Pan-African movement, which united African peoples across ethnic and national lines in a coordinated campaign that compelled European powers to grant independence","American Cold War policy that pressured European allies to decolonize as a condition of NATO membership and Marshall Plan aid","United Nations Security Council resolutions that mandated European withdrawal from African territories by 1970","The economic unviability of maintaining colonial empires after World War II, combined with rising African nationalist movements"],"c":0,"e":"Pan-Africanism: the ideology of African unity across ethnic, national, and geographic lines, provided the intellectual and political framework for coordinated decolonization. Figures like Nkrumah, Nyerere, Léopold Sédar Senghor, and Patrice Lumumba drew on Pan-African ideas to build mass independence movements. The 1958 All-African Peoples' Conference in Accra coordinated strategies across the continent. While economic factors and WWII's weakening of Europe enabled decolonization, Pan-Africanism provided the political vision that made coordinated, rapid decolonization possible.","model":{"h":"WWII decisively undermined European colonial authority: France and Belgium were occupied; Britain was exhausted and dependent on American aid; Portugal was isolated and economically weak. African soldiers who served in European armies returned with military skills, political ideas, and diminished respect for European invincibility. The 1945 Manchester Pan-African Congress demanded immediate independence.","a":"Historical atlas maps are produced for educational audiences, students, teachers, historians, and policymakers. The before-and-after format invites readers to perceive decolonization as a dramatic transformation and to ask what caused it.","p":"To document the political transformation of Africa from a colonized to a decolonized continent, enabling historical analysis of the pace, pattern, and causes of African independence movements.","pov":"Historical atlases produced in the West frame decolonization as a political process of transferring sovereignty. This perspective emphasizes formal independence while potentially underplaying the economic and political constraints that limited the meaning of that independence, neo-colonialism, debt, Cold War interference, and inherited colonial structures."}}
]},
9: { frame: "HAPP", items: [
  {"num":1,"title":"Crude Oil Prices, 1950–2000","source":"Line graph of crude oil prices (West Texas Intermediate) per barrel, adjusted for inflation, 1950–2000.","desc":"A line graph tracking the inflation-adjusted price of oil from 1950 to 2000. The price stays nearly flat from 1950 until a gray bar labeled “1973 oil embargo,” where it more than doubles almost overnight. It climbs to a peak around 1980 at roughly five times its 1950s level, falls sharply in the mid-1980s, and then rises and falls in short spikes through the 1990s.","img":"images/tl/u9-tl-4.jpg","q":"The change in oil prices shown on the graph between 1973 and 1980 most directly resulted from","o":["oil-producing states using control of a vital resource as political leverage","the collapse of the Soviet Union and the sudden end of its oil exports to the West","the spread of Green Revolution agriculture, which used huge amounts of fuel","the creation of the World Trade Organization and its new limits on oil exports"],"c":0,"e":"In 1973, Arab oil producers led by OPEC members embargoed the United States and other supporters of Israel during the Yom Kippur War, and prices jumped. The 1979 Iranian Revolution disrupted supplies again, pushing prices to their 1980 peak. The Soviet collapse (1991) and the WTO (1995) came later.","model":{"h":"After 1945, industrialized economies came to depend on cheap imported oil, much of it from the Middle East. In 1973, during the Yom Kippur War, Arab members of OPEC cut production and embargoed supporters of Israel. A second shock followed the 1979 Iranian Revolution. Both shocks caused inflation and recession in the West.","a":"Students, economists, and policymakers studying how energy prices have changed over time and how political events affected the global economy.","p":"To show long-term trends in the real price of oil and to highlight the 1973 embargo as a turning point, making visible how a political decision changed the world economy.","pov":"This is a modern data graph built from historical price records, not a source from the time. Adjusting for inflation lets it compare real prices across decades, but the graph shows only what happened to prices, not why, and it says nothing about who gained (oil producers) or who suffered (oil-importing countries)."}},
  {"num":2,"title":"Lehman Brothers Headquarters, New York, 2000s","source":"Photograph of the headquarters of Lehman Brothers, an American investment bank, on Seventh Avenue in New York City, taken before the firm’s bankruptcy in September 2008.","desc":"A glass skyscraper whose lower floors are printed with a giant world map beneath the words “Lehman Brothers.” Signs above the street level name the firm’s offices in London, New York, Hong Kong, Tokyo, Miami, and Singapore. Large video screens display the company slogan “Where vision gets built.”","img":"images/tl/u9-tl-10.jpg","q":"The details of the building shown in the photograph best illustrate which feature of the global economy in the early 2000s?","o":["The decline of New York as a center of global finance after the Cold War","Government ownership of the largest banks in the United States and Europe","Financial firms operating across many countries, linking world markets","The return of mercantilist trade policies in the West after the 1990s"],"c":2,"e":"The world map and the list of cities advertise a bank operating across continents. That global reach is exactly why Lehman’s collapse in 2008 spread a U.S. housing crisis into a worldwide financial panic. Lehman was a private firm, not government owned (B).","model":{"h":"From the 1980s onward, deregulation and new technology tied the world’s financial markets together. By the 2000s, firms like Lehman Brothers traded mortgage-backed securities worldwide. When the U.S. housing bubble burst, Lehman went bankrupt in September 2008, triggering the global financial crisis.","a":"The building’s design spoke to clients, investors, and the crowds of passersby in midtown Manhattan. The photograph itself was taken for the public record and is now used by people studying the 2008 crisis.","p":"The firm designed its headquarters to project global reach, success, and confidence. Historians use the photograph to show how globalized finance presented itself just before the crash.","pov":"The building reflects how the company wanted to be seen: worldwide, visionary, and powerful. It hides the risks the firm was taking. A historian should read the image against what happened in 2008, when that confidence proved false."}},
  {"num":3,"title":"Treating COVID-19 in an Intensive Care Unit, Brazil, 2020s","source":"Photograph of health care workers in protective equipment treating a COVID-19 patient in a hospital intensive care unit in Brazil, early 2020s.","desc":"Two health care workers wearing gowns, gloves, masks, hair covers, and face shields care for a patient receiving oxygen in a hospital bed. A heart monitor hangs on the wall above the patient, and a city of high-rise apartment buildings is visible through the window.","img":"images/tl/u9-tl-12.jpg","q":"The scene in the photograph is best understood in the context of","o":["the eradication of most infectious diseases by the late twentieth century","the spread of the 1918 influenza pandemic after the First World War","Cold War competition between the superpowers in medical technology","a pandemic that spread rapidly through global travel and trade"],"c":3,"e":"COVID-19 was first identified in Wuhan, China, in late 2019 and reached nearly every country within months, carried by air travel. Brazil became one of the hardest-hit countries. Infectious disease had not been eradicated (A); only smallpox was.","model":{"h":"COVID-19 spread worldwide in 2020, killing millions and overwhelming hospitals. Brazil suffered one of the highest death tolls in the world. The pandemic showed how global connections that carry trade and travelers also carry disease, while vaccines developed in record time revealed the power of modern medicine.","a":"News readers and the public around the world, along with public health officials. Photographs like this one were widely shared to show the strain on hospitals.","p":"To document the reality of the pandemic inside hospitals, showing the danger to patients, the protective measures health workers needed, and the pressure on medical systems.","pov":"The photographer focuses on the dedication and risk of health care workers. The image shows one patient in a well-equipped urban hospital, so it does not show the many people who lacked access to intensive care, especially in poorer areas."}},
  {"num":4,"title":"Parties to the Paris Agreement","source":"World map showing which countries are parties to the Paris Agreement on climate change, adopted in 2015.","desc":"A world map in which almost every country is shaded blue as a party to the Paris Agreement. Members of the European Union appear in a darker blue because the EU also joined as a bloc. A few countries, including Iran, Libya, and Yemen, appear in yellow as countries that had not ratified the agreement. The United States appears in a separate, lighter shade.","img":"images/tl/u9-tl-11.jpg","q":"The map best supports which conclusion about global responses to climate change?","o":["Climate change was addressed mainly by individual states acting alone, without treaties","Nearly every state joined a common framework, though some major emitters wavered","Developing countries refused to take part in international climate agreements","The European Union rejected international limits on greenhouse gas emissions"],"c":1,"e":"Nearly the whole map is shaded as parties, showing near-universal participation in one framework. The separate shading of the United States reflects that its participation changed with different presidents: it withdrew in 2020, rejoined in 2021, and moved to withdraw again in 2025.","model":{"h":"By 2015, scientists broadly agreed that greenhouse gases from fossil fuels were warming the planet. Earlier efforts such as the Kyoto Protocol (1997) had bound only wealthy countries. The Paris Agreement asked every country to set its own emissions targets.","a":"Students, journalists, and the public who want to see at a glance which countries have joined the agreement.","p":"To show how widely the agreement has been adopted and to identify the few countries outside it.","pov":"The map shows legal participation, not action. Being shaded as a party does not show whether a country actually cut its emissions, and the targets in the agreement are voluntary. A historian would need emissions data to judge how effective it was."}},
  {"num":5,"title":"NAFTA Emblem, 1994","source":"Emblem used for the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA), which joined the United States, Canada, and Mexico in a free-trade zone beginning January 1, 1994.","desc":"The flags of the United States, Mexico, and Canada are drawn as a single piece of fabric, rippling together as if they were one flag. The Mexican flag is in the center, overlapping the other two.","img":"images/tl/u9-tl-8.jpg","q":"The design of the emblem was most likely intended to","o":["present the three countries’ economies as integrated partners","celebrate a military alliance against the Soviet Union in the Cold War","announce the creation of a common currency for North America","show Mexico’s independence from foreign economic influence"],"c":0,"e":"By weaving the three flags into one, the emblem presents NAFTA as a partnership that would bind the three economies together by removing tariffs. NAFTA was a trade agreement, not a military alliance (B), and it never created a shared currency (C), unlike the euro in the EU.","model":{"h":"In the 1980s and 1990s, free-market ideas spread and regional trade blocs multiplied, including the European Union, Mercosur, and ASEAN. NAFTA removed most tariffs among the three countries. It took effect on January 1, 1994, the same day the Zapatista uprising began in Mexico in protest.","a":"Businesses, investors, and citizens of the three member countries, along with the wider world watching the new trade bloc.","p":"To promote NAFTA as a symbol of cooperation and shared prosperity among the three countries.","pov":"The emblem presents the three nations as equal partners, but it hides real inequalities between them and the debates NAFTA caused. Critics in Mexico, such as indigenous corn farmers, and labor unions in the United States and Canada argued that it would cost them their livelihoods."}}
]}
};
